# The standard logical template for every essay

Use exactly this structure.

## 1. The exact contention

One sentence only.

Example:

> Can a universal field of consciousness divide into private subjects without presupposing the very partition it was introduced to explain?

## 2. The theory’s strongest argument

Formal premises:

[
P_1,\ P_2,\ P_3
\therefore C.
]

Never attack the popularized caricature.

## 3. What the theory genuinely explains

This prevents every essay becoming polemical dismissal.

## 4. The relocation test

Ask:

[
\boxed{
\text{Which original unknown has been renamed?}
}
]

Typical relocations:

| Original problem           | New label                |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Emergence of consciousness | “Dissociation”           |
| Subject individuation      | “Limiting adjunct”       |
| Binding                    | “Integration”            |
| Manifestness               | “Intrinsic nature”       |
| Felt value                 | “Semantic information”   |
| Agency                     | “Policy selection”       |
| Temporal presence          | “Temporal processing”    |
| Mental causation           | “Supervenience”          |
| Phenomenal structure       | “Cause–effect structure” |
| World construction         | “Prediction”             |

## 5. The entailment test

Does the mechanism actually entail the target?

[
X\Rightarrow Y?
]

Or merely correlate with it?

[
X\leftrightarrow Y.
]

Or simply redescribe it?

[
X:=Y.
]

## 6. The scale test

At what scale does the theory apply?

* molecule;
* cell;
* circuit;
* brain;
* organism;
* group;
* cosmos?

Why that scale rather than another?

## 7. The token test

Does it identify one concrete subject, or only a subject-type?

## 8. The exclusion test

Why are neighbouring systems not included?

## 9. The valence test

Why does anything matter or feel good or bad?

## 10. The time test

How does the theory explain lived continuity rather than state succession?

## 11. The agency test

Does it explain authorship or merely action selection?

## 12. MCPIT comparison

Use the same final diagram each time:

[
\text{Rival mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{what it solves}
\rightarrow
\text{where it stops}.
]

Then:

[
\text{MCPIT}
\rightarrow
\text{what it retains}
\rightarrow
\text{its own unpaid debt}.
]

